  "full_title": "The Abbot Scaglia adoring the Virgin and Child",
  "short_title": "The Abbot Scaglia adoring the Virgin and Child",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "baroque",
  "material": "oil on canvas",
  "dimensions": "106.7 × 120 cm",
  "production_date": "1634-5",
  "brief_description": "Abbot Scaglia (1592--1641), cleric, diplomat, spy and one of Van Dyck’s most important patrons, commissioned this painting while suffering from ill health and in a reflective state of mind. It was meant for the church of the Recollect order of Augustinians in Antwerp; within a few years Scaglia would withdraw from public life and retire to their monastery.\n\nThe picture shows the Abbot kneeling before the Virgin and Child and receiving a blessing from the infant Christ, who seems to have paused momentarily from squirming playfully in his mother’s lap. The informality of Christ and the joyous colours contrast with the seriousness of Scaglia, intent on the salvation of his soul, and the calm gravity of the Virgin. Her face, double chin and all, appears to be a portrait, the most likely candidate being Princess Henrietta of Lorraine Phalsbourg, to whom Scaglia bequeathed the picture. Van Dyck has made a scene where the human and the divine interact with touching naturalness.",
  "accession_date": "1937-01-01",
  "current_location": "Room 21, The National Gallery (London)",
  "credit_line": "Presented by Anthony de Rothschild in memory of Louisa, Lady de Rothschild, and Constance, Lady Battersea, 1937",
